{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he became her son.</b> Pharaoh's daughter would embrace him and kiss him, and would not remove him from the royal palace, and even Pharaoh would embrace him and kiss him. And Moses would take his crown from upon his head and cast it to the ground, as a sign of what he was destined to do to him in the end: there went forth from within Pharaoh's palace one who would destroy him and his kingdom. And fire went forth from within him and consumed him, in the manner of what is written concerning Hiram king of Tyre, to whom the Holy One, blessed be He, said (Ezekiel 28:18): ״ואוציא אש מתוכך היא אכלתך ואחנך לאפר על הארץ לעיני כל ראיך״. And likewise in the future, from within Rome there is destined to go forth one who will destroy it — King Messiah — he it is of whom Isaiah prophesied (Isaiah 26:5): ״כי השח יושבי מרום קריה נשגבה ישפילנה ישפילה עד ארץ יגיענה עד עפר״, and it is written (ibid.): ״תרמסנה רגל רגלי עני פעמי דלים״, and it is written (Isaiah 27:10): ״שם ירעה עגל ושם ירבץ וכלה סעיפיה״." "<b>And she called his name Moses.</b> Bithiah daughter of Pharaoh merited through this that the name she called him was established for him forever, on account of the miracle that was wrought in Moses — that she drew him out of the water and he was saved from death. And the Holy One, blessed be He, placed this in her heart, and the Holy One, blessed be He, did not call him by any other name but this one."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:46.095992354Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}